<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilfrid Malleson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1866–1946</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> Malleson was a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> general who in 1918 led a military mission from Meshed in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to support the anti-Bolshevik Transcaspian Government. His small force of Indian and British troops fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> units along the Trans-Caspian railway, including at Dushak, before withdrawing in 1919. Soviet writers later blamed his mission for the execution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> commissars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilfrid-malleson.jpg" alt="Wilfrid Malleson" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malleson-i-413x600.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
